    "documentation": {
      "format": "markdown",
      "content": "**Scientific study:** [dataset-study-concrete-compressives-strength on GitHub](https://github.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-concrete-compressives-strength)  \n**Dataset source:** [Concrete Compressive Strength — UCI Machine Learning Repository](https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/165/concrete+compressive+strength)\n\n> **Lineage note:** The scientific results documented below — including model selection, validation and final-test metrics, diagnostics, and scientific limitations — refer to the Dataset Study and its Atlas Scientific Reproduction. The interactive prediction surface and the active Atlas release are produced by Atlas Native Training. Native Training uses a different split and execution protocol, so Native metrics and Scientific Study metrics are not directly comparable and neither lineage replaces the other.\n\nThis dataset presents a **continuous-regression** problem based on eight numerical mixture and curing-age measurements. The objective is to estimate **Concrete compressive strength** as a continuous numerical value on its original MPa scale.\n\nThe analysis is predictive rather than causal. Relationships between ingredients, aggregate quantities, water, curing age, and measured strength describe statistical structure in the observed data; they do not establish the causal effect of changing an individual input.\n\n## Dataset overview\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---:|\n| Observations | 1,030 |\n| Source columns | 9 |\n| Predictive features | 8 numerical features |\n| Distinct mixture compositions | 427 |\n| Target | `Concrete compressive strength` |\n| Target unit | MPa |\n| Problem type | Continuous regression |\n| Selected model | HistGradientBoostingRegressor |\n| Feature policy | `all_features` |\n| Primary model-selection metric | MAE |\n| Validation MAE | **2.7417 MPa** |\n| Final-test MAE | **2.5822 MPa** |\n| Final-test RMSE | 4.2104 MPa |\n| Final-test R² | 0.9387 |\n| Final-test MedAE | 1.6363 MPa |\n\nEach row represents a concrete mixture composition evaluated at one curing age, together with its measured compressive strength.\n\nThe seven mixture-component columns identify **427 distinct mixture compositions**. Many compositions are observed at more than one curing age: 181 mixtures occur across multiple rows, accounting for 784 observations. This repeated-mixture structure is important when interpreting the row-level train, validation, and final-test split.\n\nThe eight predictive features are:\n\n```text\nCement\nBlast Furnace Slag\nFly Ash\nWater\nSuperplasticizer\nCoarse Aggregate\nFine Aggregate\nAge\n```\n\nThe continuous target is:\n\n```text\nConcrete compressive strength\n```\n\nNo classification labels, positive-class semantics, thresholds, or argmax decisions are involved.\n\n## Analytical question\n\nThe analysis asks whether the eight numerical mixture-composition and curing-age variables contain sufficient predictive signal to estimate concrete compressive strength for held-out rows under the study's frozen row-level evaluation protocol.\n\nBecause mixture compositions may occur across partitions at different curing ages, this experiment does not by itself establish generalization to previously unseen mixture designs.\n\nThe fitted regressor returns one continuous prediction in MPa.\n\nThis prediction is conditional on the supplied measurements and on patterns learned from the study sample. It is not a causal estimate of how much strength would change if one ingredient or curing condition were altered.\n\n## Feature and target contract\n\n| Role | Columns |\n|---|---|\n| Numerical predictors | `Cement`, `Blast Furnace Slag`, `Fly Ash`, `Water`, `Superplasticizer`, `Coarse Aggregate`, `Fine Aggregate`, `Age` |\n| Target | `Concrete compressive strength` |\n| Target semantics | Continuous / quantitative |\n| Output unit | MPa |\n| Identifier | None provided by source |\n| Categorical predictors | None |\n\nThe target is never admitted as a predictor.\n\nBecause no observation identifier is provided, exact equality between rows or feature profiles cannot establish that records represent the same physical concrete observation.\n\n## Data quality and preparation\n\nThe source contains 1,030 rows and nine columns, and all target values are present and finite.\n\nA notable metadata discrepancy concerns `Blast Furnace Slag`: the source metadata declares an integer type, while the released table contains valid decimal measurements.\n\nThe study preserves the released measurements exactly:\n\n- 298 rows, or 28.932%, contain non-integer slag values;\n- no rounding or truncation is applied;\n- no rows are removed because of the metadata mismatch;\n- no generic imputation is introduced.\n\nRepeated observations are also preserved:\n\n| Repetition evidence | Result |\n|---|---:|\n| Exact-row groups | 11 |\n| Rows in exact-row groups | 36 |\n| Repeated feature-profile groups | 19 |\n| Rows in repeated feature-profile groups | 57 |\n| Repeated-profile groups with shared target | 10 groups / 33 rows |\n| Repeated-profile groups with target disagreement | 9 groups / 24 rows |\n\nEquality alone is not treated as proof of duplicate identity.\n\nNo generic outlier deletion, clipping, winsorization, target binning, or target transformation is applied. All eight validated predictors are retained.\n\n## Target and exploratory findings\n\n### Continuous target distribution\n\nThe target is evaluated on its original MPa scale.\n\n![Concrete compressive strength target distribution](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-concrete-compressives-strength/main/docs/images/target_distribution.png)\n\nObserved extremes and IQR-based flags are treated as descriptive diagnostics rather than automatic deletion rules.\n\n### Several predictors show measurable association with strength\n\n![Feature-to-target association ranking](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-concrete-compressives-strength/main/docs/images/feature_target_association_ranking.png)\n\nThe exploratory analysis identifies multiple predictors with meaningful univariate association to the continuous target.\n\nThese relationships do not establish independent causal importance. Their predictive value must be assessed jointly under leakage-safe fitting and evaluation procedures.\n\n### The numerical feature space contains dependence\n\n![Numerical feature correlation heatmap](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-concrete-compressives-strength/main/docs/images/numerical_feature_correlation_heatmap.png)\n\nCorrelation structure is preserved as exploratory evidence rather than converted into automatic feature deletion.\n\n### Nonlinear and interaction structure is plausible\n\n![Regression nonlinearity signals](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-concrete-compressives-strength/main/docs/images/regression_nonlinearity_signals.png)\n\n![Regression interaction signals](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-concrete-compressives-strength/main/docs/images/regression_interaction_signals.png)\n\nThe exploratory analysis records three nonlinearity signals and four interaction signals. This motivates direct comparison between an additive linear model and more flexible tree-based regression families.\n\nThe diagnostics themselves are not model-performance evidence.\n\n## Preparation and evaluation split\n\nThe study uses a reproducible shuffled, non-stratified **row-level regression split**:\n\n| Partition | Rows | Share | Purpose |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| Train | 721 | 70% | Cross-validation and hyperparameter search |\n| Validation | 154 | 15% | Frozen candidate comparison and selection |\n| Final test | 155 | 15% | Procedurally isolated final-test partition |\n\nThe split uses random seed `42`, with second-stage seed `43`.\n\nNo target bins are created for stratification.\n\nThe final-test partition remains isolated from model-family comparison, preprocessing choice, hyperparameter search, and validation selection during each execution of the scientific protocol.\n\nThis isolation is **procedural rather than mixture-group based**. Because rows are split independently, the same mixture composition can appear in fitting and held-out partitions at different curing ages.\n\nLearned numerical scaling is model-dependent and fitted only on training data. Ridge uses scaling inside its pipeline; tree-based candidates operate on the original numerical representation.\n\n## Evaluation protocol\n\n**Mean Absolute Error (MAE)** is the primary model-selection metric and is expressed in MPa.\n\nComplementary metrics are:\n\n- RMSE;\n- R²;\n- Median Absolute Error.\n\nHyperparameter selection uses training-only five-fold cross-validation:\n\n```text\nKFold\nn_splits = 5\nshuffle = True\nrandom_state = 42\n```\n\nThe practical-tie tolerance is:\n\n```text\n0.10 MPa\n```\n\nIf multiple candidates fall within that tolerance on validation MAE, the predefined tie-break order is:\n\n1. lower RMSE;\n2. lower MedAE;\n3. lower CV-MAE standard deviation;\n4. higher R²;\n5. stable model identity.\n\nThe final-test partition never participates in model selection.\n\n## Model selection\n\nThe study compares a median baseline with Ridge, Decision Tree, Random Forest, and HistGradientBoosting regression.\n\n### Candidate comparison\n\nEach trainable family is first tuned using train-only cross-validation. Its best family configuration is then frozen and evaluated once on the validation partition.\n\n| Model | MAE ↓ | RMSE ↓ | R² ↑ | MedAE ↓ | Result |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| HistGradientBoostingRegressor | **2.7417** | **4.0870** | **0.9336** | **1.8033** | **Selected** |\n| RandomForestRegressor | 3.7689 | 5.4146 | 0.8835 | 2.5385 | Candidate |\n| DecisionTreeRegressor | 4.2860 | 6.5247 | 0.8309 | 2.9000 | Candidate |\n| Ridge | 8.5944 | 10.7533 | 0.5406 | 7.1671 | Candidate |\n| Dummy median | 12.8145 | 15.8699 | -0.0005 | 11.2600 | Baseline |\n\nEvery trainable candidate improves on the median baseline.\n\nHistGradientBoostingRegressor reduces validation MAE by approximately **10.0728 MPa**, or **78.61%**, relative to the baseline.\n\nIts validation MAE is also more than 0.10 MPa below the next-best candidate, so the selection does not require a practical-tie resolution.\n\n### Candidate search configuration and hyperparameters\n\nThe four trainable families evaluate 52 hyperparameter configurations in total.\n\n| Model | Search policy | Evaluated configurations | Hyperparameters evaluated / fixed constructor |\n|---|---|---:|---|\n| Ridge | GridSearchCV | 4 | `alpha={0.1,1.0,10.0,100.0}`; numerical scaling fitted inside each training fold |\n| Decision Tree | GridSearchCV | 12 | `max_depth={3,5,8,None}`; `min_samples_leaf={1,5,10}`; fixed `random_state=42` |\n| Random Forest | GridSearchCV | 12 | `max_depth={None,12}`; `min_samples_leaf={1,3,5}`; `max_features={1.0,sqrt}`; fixed `n_estimators=300`, `random_state=42`, `n_jobs=1` |\n| HistGradientBoosting | GridSearchCV | 24 | `learning_rate={0.05,0.10}`; `max_leaf_nodes={15,31}`; `min_samples_leaf={10,20,40}`; `l2_regularization={0.0,1.0}`; fixed `max_iter=300`, `random_state=42` |\n| Dummy median | No search | 1 | `strategy=median` |\n\nThe complete search spaces and the frozen family-level winning configurations are retained in the scientific evidence and reproduced by the Atlas Scientific Reproduction.\n\nThis public documentation focuses on the candidate spaces and the selected final configuration; exact family-level execution details remain available in the scientific evidence.\n\n### Selected configuration\n\nThe selected model is HistGradientBoostingRegressor using all eight features.\n\n| Hyperparameter | Selected value |\n|---|---:|\n| `learning_rate` | 0.1 |\n| `max_leaf_nodes` | 15 |\n| `min_samples_leaf` | 10 |\n| `l2_regularization` | 1.0 |\n| `max_iter` | 300 |\n| `random_state` | 42 |\n\nNo numerical scaling is applied to the selected model.\n\n## Final holdout evaluation\n\nAfter every selection decision is frozen, the selected model is trained once on train plus validation:\n\n```text\n875 observations\n```\n\nIt is then evaluated on the procedurally isolated 155-row final-test partition.\n\n| Metric | Validation | Final test | Test − validation |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| **MAE (MPa)** | 2.7417 | **2.5822** | -0.1595 |\n| RMSE (MPa) | 4.0870 | **4.2104** | +0.1234 |\n| R² | 0.9336 | **0.9387** | +0.0050 |\n| MedAE (MPa) | 1.8033 | **1.6363** | -0.1669 |\n\nThe aggregate final-test results remain broadly consistent with validation: MAE and MedAE improve slightly, R² increases slightly, and RMSE increases modestly.\n\nThe aggregate values, however, should not be interpreted as evidence that every held-out row represents a genuinely new mixture composition. The row-level split allows mixture compositions to occur across partitions.\n\n### Error diagnostics\n\n| Diagnostic | Final-test value |\n|---|---:|\n| Residual mean | 0.7577 MPa |\n| Residual standard deviation | 4.1551 MPa |\n| Maximum absolute error | 26.6252 MPa |\n| Absolute error p50 | 1.6363 MPa |\n| Absolute error p90 | 6.4306 MPa |\n| Absolute error p95 | 7.7465 MPa |\n\nThese values describe the aggregate holdout error distribution and are not used to retune the model.\n\n## Evaluation scope: repeated mixtures\n\nThe seven mixture-component columns identify **427 distinct mixture compositions**, and many mixtures are measured at more than one curing age.\n\nBecause the scientific split is performed at the row level rather than grouping rows by mixture composition, a held-out observation can belong to a mixture that is already represented in the data used for fitting.\n\n### Validation partition\n\n| Scope | Rows | MAE | RMSE | R² |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| Mixture seen in train | 111 | 2.7199 MPa | 3.7465 MPa | 0.9477 |\n| Mixture unseen in train | 43 | 2.7978 MPa | 4.8569 MPa | 0.8763 |\n\n### Final-test partition\n\n| Scope | Rows | Share | MAE | RMSE | R² |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| Mixture seen in train + validation | 116 | 74.84% | **2.1978 MPa** | 2.9552 MPa | 0.9699 |\n| Mixture unseen in train + validation | 39 | 25.16% | **3.7255 MPa** | 6.6694 MPa | 0.8416 |\n\nOf the 155 final-test rows, **116 — approximately 74.84% — belong to mixture compositions already represented in train + validation**.\n\nThe remaining **39 rows** belong to mixture compositions not represented in the fitting partitions.\n\nPerformance is materially weaker on this smaller unseen-mixture subset:\n\n- seen-mixture MAE: **2.1978 MPa**;\n- unseen-mixture MAE: **3.7255 MPa**;\n- seen-mixture RMSE: **2.9552 MPa**;\n- unseen-mixture RMSE: **6.6694 MPa**.\n\nThis distinction is important when interpreting the headline test metrics.\n\nThe overall final-test MAE of **2.5822 MPa** primarily characterizes row-level prediction in a test set where most mixture compositions are already represented during fitting.\n\nThe unseen-mixture subset contains only 39 observations, so its metrics should also be interpreted cautiously. The study does **not** establish equivalent predictive performance for genuinely new concrete mixture designs.\n\nA dedicated grouped, prospective, or external experimental design would be required to answer that broader generalization question more strongly.\n\n## Understanding the evaluation metrics\n\n### MAE — 2.5822 MPa\n\nMAE is the average absolute difference between prediction and observed compressive strength.\n\nAcross the complete final-test sample, predictions differ from the measured target by about `2.58 MPa` on average.\n\nThis headline value combines both mixture compositions represented during fitting and mixture compositions unseen during fitting.\n\n**Lower values are better.**\n\n### RMSE — 4.2104 MPa\n\nRMSE gives greater weight to larger errors than MAE.\n\nIts value above MAE indicates that a smaller number of larger misses contribute materially to the overall error profile.\n\nThe unseen-mixture subset also shows substantially higher RMSE than the seen-mixture subset.\n\n**Lower values are better.**\n\n### R² — 0.9387\n\nR² describes predictive fit relative to a simple variance-based reference.\n\nThe value indicates strong predictive fit within this frozen row-level holdout, but it does not express error magnitude in MPa and does not establish external or new-mixture generalization.\n\n**Higher values are better.**\n\n### MedAE — 1.6363 MPa\n\nMedian Absolute Error is the median magnitude of prediction error.\n\nHalf of the final-test absolute errors are at or below approximately `1.64 MPa`.\n\n**Lower values are better.**\n\n## Scientific-study prediction and result interpretation\n\nThe frozen scientific-study model accepts the same eight numerical measurements used during training:\n\n```text\nCement\nBlast Furnace Slag\nFly Ash\nWater\nSuperplasticizer\nCoarse Aggregate\nFine Aggregate\nAge\n```\n\nIt returns one continuous numerical estimate:\n\n```text\nConcrete compressive strength\n```\n\nin:\n\n```text\nMPa\n```\n\nThe input contract rejects missing or non-finite required values.\n\n### Scientific-study illustrative inference examples\n\nThe frozen scientific-study model produces the following illustrative predictions:\n\n| Example | Predicted strength |\n|---|---:|\n| Early-age mix | 29.994813 MPa |\n| Standard mix | 46.098200 MPa |\n| Slag/fly-ash mix | 53.969705 MPa |\n| High-cement mix | 67.761977 MPa |\n\nThese values come from the scientific-study model. They are **not predictions from the active Atlas Native release**.\n\nThe examples are manually specified inputs without observed targets and therefore are not additional performance measurements.\n\nA prediction is an estimate conditional on the supplied feature values. It is not:\n\n- a guaranteed future laboratory measurement;\n- a causal estimate of changing one ingredient;\n- a substitute for physical testing;\n- evidence of equivalent validity for a new mixture design;\n- evidence of validity under a different material, laboratory, standard, or data-generating process.\n\n## Atlas lineage context\n\nAtlas maintains separate scientific and operational lineages for this dataset.\n\n### Scientific Study\n\nThe Scientific Study represents the externally developed dataset study and is the authority for:\n\n- scientific objective;\n- feature and target definition;\n- exploratory findings;\n- candidate families;\n- model-selection protocol;\n- validation metrics;\n- final-test metrics;\n- mixture-overlap diagnostics;\n- limitations and scientific interpretation.\n\n### Scientific Reproduction\n\nAtlas Scientific Reproduction independently reexecutes the frozen scientific protocol.\n\nThe current committed reproduction is classified as:\n\n```text\nreproduced_exact\n```\n\nIt reproduces:\n\n- environment compatibility;\n- source and partition identity;\n- candidate-family search;\n- family winners;\n- model-selection decisions;\n- selected configuration;\n- final fit;\n- scientific final-test metrics;\n- repeated-mixture evaluation diagnostics.\n\nThe reproduction does not convert the study into Atlas Native Training. It preserves a separate scientific lineage.\n\n### Atlas Native Training\n\nAtlas Native Training is an Atlas-owned training process used by the active Atlas release and interactive inference surface.\n\nIt uses its own split and execution protocol and does not repeat the complete scientific family-selection experiment.\n\nThe active Native release currently reports:\n\n| Metric | Atlas Native active release |\n|---|---:|\n| MAE | 2.0453 MPa |\n| RMSE | 2.9740 MPa |\n| R² | 0.9694 |\n\nThese Native values are **not directly comparable** to the Scientific Study final-test values:\n\n| Metric | Scientific Study | Atlas Native |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| MAE | 2.5822 MPa | 2.0453 MPa |\n| RMSE | 4.2104 MPa | 2.9740 MPa |\n| R² | 0.9387 | 0.9694 |\n\nThe numbers share metric names but come from different experiment lineages, partitions, and execution protocols.\n\nA lower Native MAE does not mean that the Scientific Study result should be replaced, and the Scientific Study metrics should not replace the active Native release metrics.\n\n## What this study demonstrates\n\nThe dataset provides a compact continuous-regression case with:\n\n- eight numerical predictors;\n- a continuous physical target on an interpretable unit scale;\n- source-metadata quality review;\n- repeated-row and repeated-profile ambiguity without identifiers;\n- 427 distinct mixture compositions;\n- repeated mixtures observed across different curing ages;\n- preservation rather than generic outlier cleaning;\n- procedurally isolated row-level train-validation-test separation;\n- explicit documentation of mixture overlap across partitions;\n- train-only preprocessing and hyperparameter search;\n- a median regression baseline;\n- comparison of linear, single-tree, bagging, and boosting families;\n- explicit search spaces for every candidate family;\n- validation-based model selection;\n- a final-test partition excluded from model selection;\n- aggregate residual diagnostics;\n- separate seen-mixture and unseen-mixture diagnostics;\n- reproducible scientific-study continuous inference;\n- independent Atlas Scientific Reproduction;\n- explicit separation from Atlas Native Training.\n\nThe study also shows why regression evaluation should not be reduced to a single score.\n\nMAE provides an interpretable average error in MPa, RMSE highlights larger misses, R² describes overall predictive fit, and MedAE characterizes the typical error with greater robustness to extremes.\n\nThe repeated-mixture analysis adds an equally important lesson: aggregate metrics can hide meaningful differences between held-out observations whose underlying mixture composition was already represented during fitting and observations belonging to genuinely unseen mixture compositions.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- The evaluation is based on a shuffled random snapshot rather than temporal, prospective, or external validation.\n- The split is row-level rather than mixture-grouped.\n- Approximately three quarters of final-test rows share their mixture composition with rows used for fitting.\n- The headline final-test metrics are therefore dominated by mixture compositions already represented in train + validation.\n- Only 39 final-test rows belong to mixture compositions unseen during fitting.\n- The unseen-mixture subset is too small to support a strong generalization claim about genuinely new mixture designs.\n- Generalization to new concrete mixture compositions is not established by this experiment.\n- External representativeness is not established.\n- The source provides no observation identifier.\n- Repeated rows or feature profiles therefore cannot be treated as confirmed duplicate physical observations.\n- The source metadata type for `Blast Furnace Slag` conflicts with valid released decimal measurements.\n- IQR and target-extreme flags are descriptive rather than proof of erroneous observations.\n- The complete final-test partition contains only 155 observations.\n- Individual prediction uncertainty intervals are not produced.\n- Measurement error in the source variables is not modeled.\n- Performance under different laboratories, materials, standards, environmental conditions, or populations is not established.\n- No causal effect of altering mixture components or curing age is estimated.\n- Operational feature availability and measurement stability are unconfirmed.\n- No production monitoring, drift, or retraining policy is evaluated by the scientific study.\n- Operational suitability is not established.\n\n## Responsible interpretation\n\nThe evidence supports a scoped predictive conclusion:\n\n> Within this dataset and the frozen shuffled row-level evaluation protocol, the available mixture-composition and curing-age measurements contain strong predictive information for concrete compressive strength. HistGradientBoostingRegressor achieves a final-test MAE of approximately **2.58 MPa**, RMSE of **4.21 MPa**, R² of **0.9387**, and MedAE of approximately **1.64 MPa**.\n\nThese headline metrics primarily characterize held-out rows whose mixture compositions are represented in the fitting data: **116 of the 155 final-test rows** fall into that category.\n\nFor those seen-mixture rows, MAE is approximately **2.20 MPa**.\n\nFor the **39 rows** belonging to mixture compositions unseen during fitting, MAE rises to approximately **3.73 MPa** and RMSE to approximately **6.67 MPa**.\n\nThe comparison between Ridge and the flexible tree-based candidates supports the exploratory hypothesis that nonlinear and interaction-aware models better represent the predictive structure present in this dataset under the executed protocol.\n\nThe study therefore supports effective **row-level prediction within the observed study design, particularly for mixture compositions already represented during fitting**.\n\nIt does not establish:\n\n- equivalent performance for genuinely new concrete mixture designs;\n- causal effects of changing mixture components or curing age;\n- prospective performance;\n- external generalization;\n- operational suitability.\n\nUse beyond this analytical setting would require a validation design appropriate to the intended deployment scenario, potentially including mixture-grouped evaluation, representative external data, verification of the measurement process, uncertainty assessment, distribution-shift evaluation, monitoring policies, and application-specific error tolerances."
    }
  },
  "source_draft_schema_version": "1.0.0"
}